11. Privileged functions should be capability-gated

Support functions shouldn't merely check:

Is this user authenticated?

They should check:

Identity
+
Role
+
Tenant
+
Purpose
+
Entitlement
+
Current session risk

before sensitive actions.

Authorization model:

allow(action) =
user.role
AND user.tenant
AND object.tenant
AND support_assignment
AND entitlement
AND session_trust

Failure of any condition:

DENY

12. Free-tier segmentation

Best architecture:

PUBLIC INTERNET
      ↓
FREE ACCOUNTS
      ↓
SEPARATE AUTHORIZATION BOUNDARY
      ↓
ISOLATED DATA PLANE
      ↓
NO SHARED PRIVILEGED SUPPORT PATH

Riskier:

FREE ACCOUNT
      ↓
SAME APP
      ↓
SAME SUPPORT INFRASTRUCTURE
      ↓
SAME TENANT MANAGEMENT
      ↓
PRODUCTION DATA

Whether Canvas exactly followed the second architecture is not publicly established.

But this is precisely the architectural question the incident raises.

13. Abuse-case testing

Security testing often asks:

Can an unauthenticated attacker exploit this?

But SaaS companies must also ask:

What can a malicious legitimate user do?

Test:

free account
expired account
student account
instructor account
trial administrator
support user
compromised institution admin

Then attempt:

IDOR
tenant hopping
object enumeration
support-ticket manipulation
API abuse
role escalation
impersonation
mass export

This is authenticated adversarial testing.

14. Attack breakers
Breaker 1 — Hard tenant isolation

Every request validates user tenant against target-object tenant.

Breaker 2 — Separate free-tier infrastructure

High-risk free identities have no implicit bridge into enterprise tenant functionality.

Breaker 3 — Support-function isolation

Support tooling operates through tightly scoped APIs rather than broad backend privileges.

Breaker 4 — Rate limits

A free account cannot query millions of user objects.

Breaker 5 — Behavioral analytics

Normal teacher:

20–200 users

Attacker:

2,000,000 users

That should look absurd.

Breaker 6 — Step-up authorization

Sensitive support actions require privileged employee authentication and explicit case assignment.

Breaker 7 — Immutable tenant boundary

Tenant IDs come from server-side authorization context, never attacker-controlled request parameters.

