17. “Delete the stolen data” is not a security control

Instructure said it reached an agreement with the unauthorized actor and received digital evidence represented as shred logs showing deletion, while acknowledging there was no complete certainty.

This deserves a cold technical statement:

Once data exits the trusted environment, technical control over every copy is lost.

A criminal can:

duplicate it
snapshot it
share it
retain subsets
lie

So:

ATTACKER SAYS DELETED
≠
CRYPTOGRAPHIC PROOF OF GLOBAL DELETION

That's not criticism of Instructure specifically.

It's an architectural fact.

18. Governance failure questions

These are the questions boards should be forced to answer:

Was Free-for-Teacher included in enterprise threat modeling?
Who owned its security?
Was it considered production?
What data could a free account ultimately influence?
Was support functionality penetration-tested from a malicious-user perspective?
Were cross-tenant abuse cases tested?
Did the security team have authority to shut down the free feature?
How quickly could it be disabled?
What dependency did enterprise customers have on shared infrastructure?
Why was the ultimate remediation permanent discontinuation?

We don't assert answers without evidence.

We document the questions.

